---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CopyCommand from "../../components/CopyCommand.astro";
import { installCommand, repository } from "../../lib/catalog";
---

<Layout title="Getting started" active="start">
  <div class="wrap guide-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">A small addition to your workflow</p>
    <h1>
      Your next skill.
      <br />
      <em>Ready when you are.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Skills are plain Markdown instructions your agent can follow.
      <br />
      Install the ones you need, then put them to work.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-grid">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="install">1. Choose and install</h2>
        <p>
          With Node.js and npm installed, open a terminal in your project and
          run:
        </p>
        <CopyCommand command={installCommand()} />
        <p>
          The installer lets you pick skills and choose which supported agents
          to install them for. Follow its prompts to select the installation
          scope.
        </p>
        <p>Already know the skill you want? Install just that one:</p>
        <CopyCommand command={installCommand("diagnose")} />
        <h2 id="invoke">2. Ask your agent to use it</h2>
        <p>Start with a real task and name the skill:</p>
        <CopyCommand
          command="Use the diagnose skill to investigate this failing test."
          label="Copy example prompt"
        />
        <p>
          Most skills can also load automatically when an agent recognizes a
          matching task. Some run only when you ask; others require a harness
          event. Every skill page explains its invocation mode.
        </p>
        <h2 id="adapt">3. Make the workflow yours</h2>
        <p>
          Read the installed SKILL.md. Keep the instructions that help, and
          adapt the workflow to your project. You can begin with one skill and
          add more as you need them.
        </p>
        <h2 id="manual">Prefer a manual install?</h2>
        <p>
          Copy the entire skill directory from the repository into your agent’s
          skills directory. Include bundled scripts, references, and assets when
          present. Check your agent’s documentation for its supported location.
        </p>
        <p>
          For Claude Code, a personal skill directory can live at{" "}
          <code>~/.claude/skills/&lt;skill-name&gt;/</code>. For a local
          checkout, this repository also includes{" "}
          <code>scripts/link-skills.sh</code>.
        </p>
        <h2 id="questions">Common questions</h2>
        <details>
          <summary>Which agent can I use?</summary>
          <p>
            The skills installer supports agents including Claude Code, Codex,
            and Cursor. The instructions are portable Markdown, but individual
            skills may require additional tools. Read each skill’s instructions
            before using it.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I install every skill?</summary>
          <p>
            Start with a specific task. Install a skill for that task, learn its
            workflow, and expand your toolkit when it helps.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if my agent doesn’t find the skill?</summary>
          <p>
            Check that you installed it for the agent and scope you’re using.
            Start a new session if your agent requires it, then name the skill
            explicitly. Review the installer’s output for the destination
            directory.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How is the repository licensed?</summary>
          <p>
            The repository uses the PolyForm Shield 1.0.0 license. Some skills
            include third-party material with separate attribution. Review the{" "}
            <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>license</a> and{" "}
            <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`}>
              third-party notices
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </details>
      </article>
      <aside class="guide-aside">
        <p class="eyebrow">On this page</p>
        <a href="#install">Choose and install</a>
        <a href="#invoke">Use a skill</a>
        <a href="#adapt">Make it yours</a>
        <a href="#manual">Manual installation</a>
        <a href="#questions">Common questions</a>
        <hr />
        <p>Have a task in mind?</p>
        <a class="button" href="/#library">
          Find a skill →
        </a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
